WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:14.110
Pancho Villa was one of the most divisive
figures in history.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:19.880
To millions he was the Mexican Robin Hood,
to others a ruthless terrorist who killed

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.380
without conscience.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.320
What is without doubt is that he rose from
humble beginnings to become one of the most

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.590
famous Mexicans in history, playing a vital
role in the decade that shaped modern Mexico.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:36.710
In this week’s Biographics, we discover
the real story of Pancho Villa.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:43.730
Beginnings
There is a lot of mystery surrounding the

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.300
early life of the man who became Pancho Villa.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.610
Most historians believe that he was born on
June 5th 1878 in the tiny village of Rancho

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:55.180
de la Coyotada in the state of Durango, Mexico.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:59.239
His birth name was Jose Doroteo Arango Arambula.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:03.090
When he was a few years old, his father, Agustin
Arango, died.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:08.000
This meant that Doroteo, the oldest of five
children, had to start working on the hacienda.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.750
Getting up at three o’clock in the morning,
he had to walk for ten miles to start work

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:12.750
at five.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.380
The work consisted of planting corn and running
errands.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:20.250
Villa later claimed that he never attended
a day of school in his life.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.510
When not working, he liked to play cards and
was often getting into fights with his peers.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:29.610
During his teens he held a number of jobs
including muleskinner, bricklayer and butcher.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.470
Then, at the age of 16, he moved to Chihuahua
for work.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:36.820
The story goes that two years later he returned
to his home town in order to hunt down a man

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.590
named Agustin Lopez Negrete, who had raped
his sister.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.280
He apparently found the rapist and killed
him.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.380
This episode is said to have set him on his
life of crime.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.380
He fled to the Sierra Madre mountains and
joined a group of bandits, changing his name

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.950
to Pancho Villa.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:56.350
The bandits, who numbered between 40 and 50
men, raided the ranches of the wealthy to

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:57.630
steal cattle and horses.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.100
They lived in the mountains and trained as
guerrilla fighters.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.939
Their reputation as fighters against the system
established them as freedom fighters in the

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:09.950
minds of the poor, who gave them shelter and
warned them when the rurales, or rural police

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:10.950
force, were closing in.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.970
In 1902, Villa was captured by the rurales.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:17.230
According to the practice of the times, he
was inducted into the Federal Army.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:21.730
He spent several months in the army and then
deserted, heading back into the hills to rejoin

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:22.730
his gang.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.700
Around this time, he killed an army officer,
which made him one of the most wanted men

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.940
in northern Mexico.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.070
A reward of ten thousand pesos was placed
on his head.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:36.220
Pancho became the leader of his bandit group
and continued to lead increasingly violent

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:37.220
raids upon the wealthy.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.780
The legend says that he would sometimes risk
capture by walking openly through the streets

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.270
of Chihuahua to distribute money to the poor.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:49.170
In so doing, he won the loyalty of the people,
who helped to protect him from the authorities.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:57.350
Revolution
In 1910, Mexico seemed to be a model of democratic

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.350
stability.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:02.330
Porfiro Diaz had been president for 30 years
and the country was celebrating 100 years

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:03.330
of independence.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:08.430
But Diaz ruled with an iron fist and the elections
that were held every four years were merely

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.350
a façade to appease foreign governments.

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.800
Ninety percent of the country lived in poverty.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.970
Since 1906, there had been a number of worker
rebellions against the conditions they were

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:20.530
forced to exist under.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:23.489
Each one had been forcibly put down by the
Diaz regime.

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:28.080
A number of intellectuals began writing against
the regime, including a man named Francisco

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.130
Madero, who decided to run for President in
1910.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:36.540
When his campaign began to gather momentum,
however, President Diaz had Madero arrested

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.570
and thrown in prison.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:41.920
The mock election went ahead and Diaz was
re-elected for the seventh time.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.440
Madero managed to escape from prison and fled
to the United States.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.620
From there he began planning for revolution.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:52.720
Meanwhile, down in Chihuahua, Pancho Villa
had established a reputation as a kind of

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.080
Mexican Robin Hood, a champion of the underprivileged.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:02.950
But he was also known as a wild man, a ruthless
killer who would shed blood with impunity.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:08.299
Seeking respectability, he met with a supporter
of Francisco Madero by the name of Abraham

00:04:08.299 --> 00:04:09.400
Gonzalez.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.270
By aligning himself with Madero, Gonzalez
told Pancho, he would be able to re-invent

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.070
himself as a legitimate man of the people.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:21.449
Villa agreed and he threw the support of his
bandit group behind the revolutionary cause.

00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:25.610
By now Pancho had developed a strong hatred
for the regime of Porfiro Diaz, along with

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:28.970
the exploitary landowners and the rurales
who had been hunting him for more than a decade.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:34.659
He and his men brought skills to the revolutionary
cause that had been honed over years of raiding,

00:04:34.659 --> 00:04:36.680
fighting and evading the authorities.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:41.559
In battle, Pancho fought like a man possessed
and now that he had an established cause,

00:04:41.559 --> 00:04:42.689
he was even more ferocious.

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:47.430
He also proved himself as a master of military
tactics, brilliantly marshaling his small

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.270
band against much larger government forces.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:54.039
On one occasion, at the battle of Escobas,
Pancho put hats upon planted sticks in order

00:04:54.039 --> 00:04:58.190
to fool the rurales into thinking that he
had a much larger army than he actually did.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:02.229
By the beginning of 1911, that army had grown
to more than 400 people and was racking up

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:04.590
decisive victories over the Federal Army at
such places as Naica, Camargo, and Pilar de

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:05.590
Conchos.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.949
In early 1911, there was a challenge to the
revolutionary leadership of Madero from the

00:05:08.949 --> 00:05:11.449
ultra-radical Mexican Liberal Party.

00:05:11.449 --> 00:05:15.960
To put down the challenge, Madero turned to
Pancho, who moved in and arrested the leaders

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:16.960
of the party.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.770
A grateful Madero rewarded Villa by making
him a colonel of his revolutionary army.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:26.620
In May, 1911, Madero’s 3000 strong force,
with Villa at the forefront, laid siege to

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.580
the key border city of Ciudad Juarez.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:33.199
The town was guarded by 500 Federal troops,
along with 300 civilian auxiliaries and local

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:34.199
police.

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:37.539
The fighting went on for two days at the end
of which the Federals surrendered.

00:05:37.539 --> 00:05:42.690
On May 25th, with his armies being defeated
and public opinion unanimously against him,

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.029
President Diaz resigned and fled into exile.

00:05:45.029 --> 00:05:49.749
A free and fair election was held in which
Francisco Madero was elected President.

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:54.440
Madero signed the Treaty of Ciudad Juarez
by which the Federal Army would remain in

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:55.440
place.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.960
He, therefore, ordered the disbanding of his
rebel army to return to their pre-war lives.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.310
Madero allowed the structure of the Diaz government
to remain.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:07.589
To Pancho Villa this was a fatal error, and
he let his feelings be known, telling Madero

00:06:07.589 --> 00:06:10.139
. . .
You sir, have destroyed the revolution.

00:06:10.139 --> 00:06:14.300
It’s simple; this bunch of dandies have
made a fool of you, and this will eventually

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.940
cost us our necks, yours included.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:25.399
Division
Villa and his fellow revolutionaries were

00:06:25.399 --> 00:06:31.059
also incensed that Madero refused to distribute
seized hacienda land to veterans of the struggle

00:06:31.059 --> 00:06:33.089
as payment for services rendered.

00:06:33.089 --> 00:06:37.619
Villa became increasingly critical of Madero’s
actions, especially his putting a former supporter

00:06:37.619 --> 00:06:40.699
of deposed President Diaz as Minister of War.

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:45.229
Even more outspoken, was a fellow revolutionary
leader by the name of Pascual Orozco.

00:06:45.229 --> 00:06:49.099
In March, 1912, Orozco led a rebellion against
the new regime.

00:06:49.099 --> 00:06:51.139
Madero called on Villa to put it down.

00:06:51.139 --> 00:06:56.419
Despite being urged by Orozco to join the
revolt, Pancho led his army of 400 in conjunction

00:06:56.419 --> 00:07:01.919
with Federal forces in a series of decisive
victories that quashed the rebellion.

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:06.029
Following the victory against Orozco, Villa’s
band joined up with Federal militia in the

00:07:06.029 --> 00:07:08.439
city of Torreon in the state of Coahuila.

00:07:08.439 --> 00:07:11.940
The Federal commander, General Victoriano
Huerta, detested Villa.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:16.869
He was initially welcoming, showering him
with praise and awarding him an honorary generalship.

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:22.080
But the pleasantries didn’t last long, with
Huerta accusing Pancho of stealing a horse.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:25.639
With his honor publicly impugned, Villa responded
by slapping the general.

00:07:25.639 --> 00:07:29.809
This was the pretext that Huerta was after
and he promptly had Villa arrested and charged

00:07:29.809 --> 00:07:31.129
with insubordination.

00:07:31.129 --> 00:07:33.270
An execution by firing squad was ordered.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:37.809
Word of the affair reached President Madero
who telegraphed Huerta and ordered him not

00:07:37.809 --> 00:07:40.139
to execute Villa but to keep him as a prisoner.

00:07:40.139 --> 00:07:42.919
Villa was sent to the Belem Prison in Mexico
City.

00:07:42.919 --> 00:07:47.199
While incarcerated there he learned to read
and write under the guidance of a fellow prisoner.

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:50.400
In June, he was transferred to a prison in
Santiago.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.679
Six months later he escaped, jumping onboard
a train bound for the United States.

00:07:54.679 --> 00:07:59.379
On January 2nd, 1913 he arrived in El Paso,
Texas.

00:07:59.379 --> 00:08:03.080
While in prison, Pancho had learned of a planned
coup against Madero.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:07.800
He attempted to get a warning message through
to the president, but it never arrived in

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:08.800
time.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.069
On February 22nd, 1913, Madero was gunned
down on the orders of General Huerta, the

00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:16.620
man who had come so close to executing Villa
a year earlier.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:20.149
Huerta now installed himself as the President
of Mexico.

00:08:20.149 --> 00:08:22.599
Villa was incensed at this series of events.

00:08:22.599 --> 00:08:27.210
Even though he had become disillusioned with
his actions upon gaining power, he still viewed

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:31.409
Francisco Madero as the apostle of democracy
and his personal role model.

00:08:31.409 --> 00:08:36.149
Now that Huerta, his personal enemy, was in
power he knew that he had to return to Mexico.

00:08:36.149 --> 00:08:40.650
He felt the mantle of completing the dream
of democracy that Madero had instilled in

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.520
him was upon his shoulders.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:09:24.110
Okay, now just before we get into Villa’s
really brutal phase, it’s time or a quick

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:39.240
word from our sponsor Scribd.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:10:14.210
Deposing Presidents
The Mexican people were just as upset as Villa

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:24.350
at the turn of events.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:28.649
Except for the wealthy businessmen who were
relieved by this ‘return to order’, the

00:10:28.649 --> 00:10:33.300
vast majority viewed Huerta as a ‘jackal’
who had stolen the Presidency.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:38.779
In March of 1913, Pancho Villa returned to
Mexico intent on avenging the murder of Madero

00:10:38.779 --> 00:10:42.269
and relieving his country of the tyranny of
Huerta.

00:10:42.269 --> 00:10:45.121
He later recalled . . .
That night I had eight men with me, and we

00:10:45.121 --> 00:10:50.740
had no definite plan but had decided to make
for my old haunts in the Sierra Madre mountains,

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.090
where I knew I could find men to follow me.

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.370
We had one sack of flour, two small packages
of coffee and some salt.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:58.040
That was all our food supply.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:01.459
Of course, we were all well armed, but had
little ammunition.

00:11:01.459 --> 00:11:05.709
Villa’s small band began making lightning
raids upon the Federal forces that were garrisoned

00:11:05.709 --> 00:11:08.149
in Northern Mexican towns and forts.

00:11:08.149 --> 00:11:11.470
Slowly the were able to accumulate the arms
and supplies that they needed.

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:15.720
Within a week, Villa’s band had grown to
more than a hundred men and within three months

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:19.500
it had morphed into a legitimate army of 18,000
troops.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:22.600
In battle, Villa proved to be ruthless.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.320
His standard policy was to kill prisoners.

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:28.050
A lucky few would only be spared by the intervention
of his lieutenants.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:32.949
In contrast to this brutal side of his nature,
though, Pancho had a strong social conscience.

00:11:32.949 --> 00:11:36.850
For two months in 1913, he held the position
of Governor of Chihuahua.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:41.660
He expropriated land from the wealthy to distribute
to the poor, opened several schools and enacted

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:43.899
laws to protect widows and orphans.

00:11:43.899 --> 00:11:48.490
On Christmas Day, 1913, he gathered the poor
of Chihuahua together and gave them each fifteen

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:49.490
pesos.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:53.160
By such actions, he became the hero and savior
of the underclass.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.750
By early 1914, Villa had control over the
northern part of Mexico.

00:11:56.750 --> 00:12:01.709
Handing over the governorship to his lieutenant,
he began the march south to Mexico City where

00:12:01.709 --> 00:12:04.230
Huerta sat in the presidential chair.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:08.800
As he swept south, he conquered everything
in his way, gaining a reputation as the Centaur

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:09.800
of the North.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.819
By June his army had grown to 50,000, most
of whom were on horseback.

00:12:14.819 --> 00:12:19.430
By now it was a fully-fledged army, with mobile
hospitals and recruits from other countries,

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.160
including the United States.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:26.020
At the head of this, the largest revolutionary
army in the history of the Americas, stood

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:31.990
Pancho Villa, a leader with great charisma
who at the same earned the respect and fear

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:32.990
of his men.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:36.810
Villa developed a military strategy that proved
enormously successful.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:41.660
Day and night, he would pour a continuous
frontal assault against the enemy line, supported

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:42.970
by cavalry troops.

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:47.450
The attacks were continually rolling with
troops at the front being replaced by those

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:48.450
behind.

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:52.750
This gave the defenders no time to rest, resulting
in them almost always being overwhelmed by

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:55.150
the sustained force of the attack.

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:58.800
Villa also established offices within his
army similar to government departments.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:02.639
He had an office of foreign affairs, who were
particularly focused on reaching out to President

00:13:02.639 --> 00:13:04.379
Woodrow Wilson for US aid.

00:13:04.379 --> 00:13:07.639
His financial committee made contact with
wealthy landowners to assure them that they

00:13:07.639 --> 00:13:10.110
would be mistreated once victory was achieved.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:14.939
The United States offered support to the revolutionary
cause through a round-about means.

00:13:14.939 --> 00:13:19.160
Villa sold livestock and other products to
the US, which provided the funds with which

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:21.569
to purchase American weapons and ammunition.

00:13:21.569 --> 00:13:25.880
It was this flow of weaponry that enabled
the rebel army to match up to the Federal

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:26.880
forces.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:31.129
In recognition of the aid being received from
north of the border, Vila ensured that no

00:13:31.129 --> 00:13:33.050
American property was confiscated.

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:38.040
Positive press in American media resulted,
with the Washington Times reporting in January,

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:43.920
1914 about Villa that ’he is now carrying
on a campaign of modern and humane warfare’.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:47.439
The media, in fact, fell in love with Villa,
with one editor writing . . .

00:13:47.439 --> 00:13:52.899
Villa is without the possibility of any doubt
the greatest leader Mexico has ever had.

00:13:52.899 --> 00:13:55.990
His method fighting is astonishingly like
Napoleon’s.

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:59.540
Villa instituted some telling innovations
in Mexican warfare.

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:04.220
He was the first to leave the women and children
behind and force the pace of his army with

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:06.720
raids, marching far away from his base.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:10.990
He would abandon his supply trains and head
far out in search of the enemy.

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:15.699
He was also the first to stage night time
attacks, which became especially devastating

00:14:15.699 --> 00:14:16.699
to the enemy.

00:14:16.699 --> 00:14:20.680
In the spring of 1914, the two key cities
of Torreon and Zacatecas were taken.

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:25.749
These victories brought the regime to its
knees, with Huerta fleeing into exile on July,

00:14:25.749 --> 00:14:26.990
14th, 1914.

00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:31.089
The Federal Army collapsed, leaving the revolutionaries
to fill the vacuum.

00:14:31.089 --> 00:14:35.809
Into the presidency now stepped Venustiano
Carranza, a former hacienda owner who brought

00:14:35.809 --> 00:14:38.009
his forces into Mexico City ahead of Villa.

00:14:38.009 --> 00:14:41.610
Yet, it was Pancho Villa who was the hero
of the revolution.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.399
Wherever he went, people would yell out ‘Viva
Villa!’

00:14:43.399 --> 00:14:48.410
Ousting Carranza
The struggle, however, was not yet over.

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:53.980
Villa and his fellow revolutionary leader,
Emiliano Zapata joined forces to oust Carranza,

00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:56.509
who they had never intended to take the helm.

00:14:56.509 --> 00:15:02.800
On December 6th, 1914, Villa rode at the head
of 50,000 men, alongside Zapata with his 15,000.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.050
By then Carranza had already fled the capital.

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:07.899
Pancho Villa was now at the height of his
power.

00:15:07.899 --> 00:15:12.500
Wearing a navy blue suit and a cap with the
eagle of the Mexican flag, he rode through

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:16.339
the city as the masses turned out to shower
him with praise.

00:15:16.339 --> 00:15:20.699
He went to the presidential palace and sat
on the Presidential chair, but only long enough

00:15:20.699 --> 00:15:21.970
to have his picture taken.

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:25.680
He never served as the President of Mexico,
though he could have easily won an election

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:26.680
at that time.

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:31.790
A council between the various fighting factions
failed to bring a consensus and Villa continued

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:34.279
to lead his army against pockets of rebellion.

00:15:34.279 --> 00:15:39.100
Alvaro Obregon was a supporter of Carranza
who won a series of decisive battles against

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:40.139
Villa’s army.

00:15:40.139 --> 00:15:43.790
Pancho was outsmarted by Obregon and many
of his men were killed.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:46.889
He was forced to retreat to his base in the
northern Sierra Madre mountains.

00:15:46.889 --> 00:15:51.980
In a few months of fierce fighting the invincible
army that had marched into Mexico City had

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:57.350
been reduced to a rag tag mob of just a few
hundred men.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:16:04.829
Violent Streak
A change now came over Pancho Villa.

00:16:04.829 --> 00:16:06.691
His violent streak was fully unleashed.

00:16:06.691 --> 00:16:11.110
In the time of San Pedro de la Cueva, he brought
all of the men in town into the middle of

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:15.190
the town and killed them all, personally shooting
the local priest in the head.

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:18.870
Then, in Santa Isabella, he shot several American
miners.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:23.990
The Americans now distanced themselves from
Villa and recognized the reinstituted Carranza

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:24.990
government.

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:29.319
At round 4 in the morning on the March 9th,
1916, Villa, incensed that President Wilson

00:16:29.319 --> 00:16:33.410
had legitimized the Carranza government, led
a force of one hundred men across the border

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:36.059
and into the town of Columbus, New Mexico.

00:16:36.059 --> 00:16:41.420
They attacked the town of 600 inhabitants
from four directions, shooting indiscriminately,

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:43.029
looting and burning buildings.

00:16:43.029 --> 00:16:45.939
Villa, however, had chosen the wrong town.

00:16:45.939 --> 00:16:50.959
The 13th US Cavalry was stationed there, with
4 machine guns and 400 highly trained soldiers.

00:16:50.959 --> 00:16:56.029
The majority of his men were killed, but Villa
managed to get away, leaving a trail of destruction

00:16:56.029 --> 00:16:57.059
in his wake.

00:16:57.059 --> 00:17:01.130
The American response to the unprovoked Columbus
attack as overwhelming.

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:06.750
10,000 troops were put on trains and sent
to the border with the single objective of

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:08.430
capturing the fugitive Pancho Villa.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:13.230
At the head of the American forces was John
Pershing, who led his troops, along with Apache

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:18.120
Indian scouts 350 miles across the border
in search of Villa.

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:22.370
Into every village that he went, Pershing
would yell out, ‘Where is Pancho Villa?’

00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:24.970
Every time the locals would provide false
information.

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:29.700
On several occasions portions of Pershing’s
army skirmished with Villa and his tiny band.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:32.059
On one occasion, Pancho was shot in the leg.

00:17:32.059 --> 00:17:37.150
In April, American newspapers reported that
he had died as a result of the leg being infected.

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:41.530
The reality was that Villa was still very
much alive, though badly wounded and hiding

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:43.380
out in a cave in the Sierra Madres.

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:47.850
Pershing’s expedition managed to capture
weaponry and horses but failed in its objective

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:49.460
of bringing Pancho in.

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:53.340
With the American’s having withdrawn, Villa
reverted to his guerrilla fighting ways.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:58.380
He still had the support of the people and
was determined to continue fighting against

00:17:58.380 --> 00:17:59.380
Carranza’s regime.

00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:04.250
Then, in 1920, with another coup in the offing,
President Carranza was killed as he fled to

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:07.250
the Gulf of Mexico with the national treasure.

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:11.190
Villa saw this turn of events as his opportunity
to come to a peace settlement with the new

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:12.190
regime.

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:14.660
He rode for 13 days to the city of Sabinas,
Coahuila and telegraphed new president Adolfo

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:16.850
de la Huerta requesting an amnesty.

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:21.870
Huerta agreed, grateful that the threat of
a Villa overthrow was nullified.

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:30.710
Retirement and Death
Vila was given a 25,000 acre farm in Canutillo.

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:35.320
He brought 200 of his men with him, who he
immediately put to work on improving the run-down

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:36.320
property.

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:40.430
Within a short time, Canutillo was turned
into a commune, with a school, wheat, corn

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:43.610
and bean crops that were able to support a
large community of people.

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:47.559
Villa got up early each day to supervise the
work and spend time in the school house.

00:18:47.559 --> 00:18:51.810
He hardly ever left the farm, fearful of assassination
attempts.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:55.350
On the morning of July 20th, 1923 Villa did
leave his ranch.

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:59.980
He went to Parral to visit one of his wives
and to withdraw money to pay his workers.

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:05.480
As his Dodge vehicle was driving through a
deserted part of town, dozens of snipers opened

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:11.360
up, filling the car with more than forty bullet
holes, nine of which smashed into Pancho Villa’s

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:12.360
body.

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.110
His death was instantaneous.

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:16.279
Yet even in death, Villa did not find peace.

00:19:16.279 --> 00:19:20.750
Three years after his burial in Parral, his
tomb was ransacked, the corpse removed and

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:22.149
Pancho’s head cut off.

00:19:22.149 --> 00:19:25.120
Souvenir hunters had also taken pieces of
the coffin and some of his bones.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:29.399
In 2010, the Wall Street Journal reported
that his right index finger was being offered

00:19:29.399 --> 00:19:31.860
in a pawn shop in El Paso.

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:49.460
The asking price was $9,500.

